n\nTo rewrite this code to use the `let ... else` construct, you'd replace it with the following:\n\n```rust\nlet Ok(ent) = cfg.get_entry(key) else { return Ok(None) };\n```\n\nThis change directly captures the `Ok` variant from the `Result` returned by `cfg.get_entry(key)`, while the `else` clause handles the `Err` case.\n\nHowever, you've mentioned that you encountered a compilation error with the suggested syntax. If you are getting a compilation error, it may be due to using a Rust version that does not properly support this syntax, which was introduced in Rust 1.65. You can verify the minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) for your project to ensure compatibility.\n\nIf your Rust version does not support `let ... else`, you can maintain the `match` statement but add a comment to disable the Clippy lint for that specific line if removing the `pedantic` lint is not an option. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```rust\nlet entry_res = cfg.get_entry(key);\n\n// Allow manual match for Clippy warning\n#[allow(clippy::manual_let_else)]\nlet entry = match entry_res {\n    Ok(ent) => ent,\n    Err(_) => return Ok(None),\n};\n```\n\nThis way, you will suppress the lint warning while keeping the existing structure of your code intact.\n\nMake sure you are aware of the compatibility and the Clippy version you are using. You can report or check for bugs like the one you linked to if issues persist ([SO-77212336]).\n\nLet me know if you need further assistance!", "llm_model": "gpt-4o-mini", "cosine_similarity": 0.6445289254188538, "has_citation": true, "cited_source_ids": [77212336], "has_valid_citation": true, "valid_cited_source_ids": [77212336], "all_candidate_question_ids": [62541586, 6588899, 52302121, 77212336, 62541586, 23837781, 24190340, 69048869, 44209066, 65254341, 28508134, 70497554, 64705654, 73907306, 59870881, 52266912, 65257632, 53011443, 4660715, 23958040, 43563378, 72483544, 61056408, 44209066, 3475404, 68557714, 77488601, 3085070, 53011443, 58007793, 4660715, 57