{"question_id": 20443560, "title": "How to Practically Ship GLSL Shaders with your C++ Software", "tags": "<c++><opengl><glsl>", "n_tokens": 535, "view_count": 51848, "question_score": 70, "accepted_answer_id": 20443864, "ground_truth_answer": "<p>There is just \"store them directly in the executable\" or \"store them in (a) separate file(s)\", with nothing in-between. If you want a self-contained executable, putting them into the binary is a good idea. Note that you can add them as resources or tweak your build system to embed the shader strings from separate development files into source files to make development easier (with the possible addition of being able to directly load the separate files in development builds).</p>\n\n<p>Why do you think shipping the shader sources would be a problem? There is simply no other way in the GL. The precompiled binaries are only useful for caching the compilation results on the target machine. With the fast advances of GPU technology, and changing GPU architectures, and different vendors with totally incompatible ISAs, precompiled shader binaries do not make sense at all.</p>\n\n<p>Note that putting your shader sources in the executeable does not \"protect\" them, even if you encrypt them. A user can still hook into the GL library and intercept the sources you specify to the GL. And the GL debuggers out there do exactly that. </p>\n\n<p><strong>UPDATE 2016</strong></p>\n\n<p>At SIGGRAPH 2016, the OpenGL Architecture Review Board released the <a href=\"https://www.opengl.org/registry/specs/ARB/gl_spirv.txt\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>GL_ARB_gl_spirv</code></a> extension. This will allow a GL inmplementation to use the <a href=\"https://www.opengl.org/registry/specs/ARB/gl_spirv.txt\" rel=\"noreferrer\">SPIRV</a> binary intermediate language. This has some potential benefits:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Shaders can be pre-\"compiled\" offline (the final compilation for the target GPU still happens by the driver later). You don't have to ship the shader source code, but only the binary intermediate representation.</li>\n<li>There is one standard compiler frontend (<a href=\"https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glslang\" rel=\"noreferrer\">glslang</a>) which does the parsing, so differences between the parsers of different implementations can be eliminated.</li>\n<li>More shader languages can be added, without the need to change the GL implementations.</li>\n<li>It somewhat increases portability to vulkan.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>With that scheme, GL is becoming more similar to D3D and Vulkan in that regard. However, it doesn't change the greater picture. The SPIRV bytecode can still be intercepted, disassembled and decompiled. It does make reverse-engineering a little bit harder, but not by much actually. In a shader, you usually can't afford extensive obfuscuation measures, since that dramatically reduces performance - which is contrary to what the shaders are for. </p>\n\n<p>Also keep in mind that this extension is not widely available right now (autumn 2016). And Apple has stopped supporting GL after 4.1, so this extension will probably never come to OSX.</p>\n\n<p><strong>MINOR UPDATE 2017</strong> </p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://www.opengl.org/registry/specs/ARB/gl_spirv.txt\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><code>GL_ARB_gl_spirv</code></a> is now official core feature of <a href=\"https://khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/specs/gl/glspec46.core.pdf\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenGL 4.6</a>, so that we can expect growing adoption rate for this feature, but it doesn't change the bigger picture by much.</p>\n", "retrieved_context": [{"answer_id": 51298240, "question_id": 51297676, "question_title": "Best practices for coding , compiling, debugging and maintaining shader programs in opengl?", "chunk_text": "Q: Best practices for coding , compiling, debugging and maintaining shader programs in opengl?\nA: <blockquote>\n  <p>I'm using visual studio. Is there any tools or extensions for it to support opengl shaders?</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>There is a <a href=\"https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=DanielScherzer.GLSL\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">GLSL Integration</a> at Visual Studio Marketplace. I generally make a separate folder for shaders to organize them.</p>\n\n<p>Its a bit hard and frustrating to debug shader code, but with practice and multiple iterations of debugging sessions it becomes more natural. I have started to experiment with my shaders at <a href=\"https://www.shadertoy.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Shadertoy</a> before porting them back at GLSL, which is surprisingly trivial.</p>\n\n<p>Having said that, there are some cool tools you can use to debug your graphics, namely <a href=\"https://renderdoc.org/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">RenderDoc</a> or <a href=\"https://gpuopen.com/compute-product/codexl/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">AMD CodeXL</a></p>\n\n<p>A friend suggested that I add <a href=\"https://github.com/ValveSoftware/vogl\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">vogl</a> to the list of debuggers too. I have not used it, but heard good praises.</p>\n", "score": 0.7049956917762756}, {"answer_id": 12711985, "question_id": 11270231, "question_title": "GLSL - varying variable gets garbage value - GL_POINTS", "chunk_text": "Q: GLSL - varying variable gets garbage value - GL_POINTS\nA: <p>I've just had some similar problems with an old ATI card.</p>\n\n<p>You could try calling <code>glTexEnvf( GL_POINT_SPRITE_ARB, GL_COORD_REPLACE_ARB, GL_TRUE );</code> in your main program - that fixed gl_PointCoord for me.</p>\n\n<p>I'm not sure if this would work for you (I don't know why it worked for me...) but my varying garbling problem was fixed by adding the line <code>_coord = gl_TexCoord[0].xy;</code> to the end of my fragment shader (_coord is an otherwise unused vec2 and I didn't call gl_TexCoord anywhere else at all). I found this suggested on a forum, but for the life of me I can't find it again!</p>\n", "score": 0.5896499752998352}, {"answer_id": 63100712, "question_id": 63092561, "question_title": "Optimize GLSL for half float", "chunk_text": "Q: Optimize GLSL for half float\nA: <p>While GLSL does not have a <code>half</code> type, it does have <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Type_Qualifier_(GLSL)#Precision_qualifiers\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">precision qualifiers</a> whose effects are exclusive-to and dependent-on mobile platforms. I'm assuming that the (complete) optimized shader code from your example contains a default qualifier setting floats to mediump like so:\n<code>precision mediump float;</code> Note though that the actual precision remains unspecified, a <code>mediump</code> float might have 16 bits on one platform while it has 24 bits on another.</p>\n<p><strong>Here's the catch though</strong>: As stated in the linked article and the <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/specs/gl/GLSLangSpec.4.50.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">GLSL specification</a> precision qualifiers are only supported for portability and ought to have no effect on desktop platforms. That means that even desktop GPUs with float16 support would break with the specification if they honored the precision qualifier. On desktop platforms you'll have to enable and use the appropriate extension(e.g. <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/extensions/AMD/AMD_gpu_shader_half_float.txt\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>GL_AMD_gpu_shader_half_float</code></a>) and its specific syntax(e.g. types) to utilize the float16 capabilities.</p>\n", "score": 0.580335259437561}, {"answer_id": 53605507, "question_id": 53601759, "question_title": "GLSL vars conversion problem from ShaderToy to Android Studio GLSL", "chunk_text": "Q: GLSL vars conversion problem from ShaderToy to Android Studio GLSL\nA: <p><strong>NONE</strong> of the GLSL variables you mentioned are system vars</p>\n\n<p>They are user made up variables.</p>\n\n<pre><code>uniform vec2 resolution;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>has absolutely no more meaning than;</p>\n\n<pre><code>uniform vec2 foobar;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Those are variables chosen by <strong>you</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>You set them by looking up their location</p>\n\n<p>In WebGL/JavaScript</p>\n\n<pre><code>const resolutionLocation = gl.getUniformLocation(someProgram, \"resolution\");\nconst foobarLocation = gl.getUniformLocation(someProgram, \"foobar\");\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>In Java</p>\n\n<pre><code>int resolutionLocation = GLES20.glGetUniformLocation(mProgram, \"resolution\");\nint foobarLocation = GLES20.glGetUniformLocation(mProgram, \"foobar\");\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>You set them in WebGL/JavaScript</p>\n\n<pre><code>gl.useProgram(someProgram);  \ngl.uniform2f(resolutionLocation, yourVariableForResolutionX, yourVariableForResolutionY);\ngl.uniform2f(foobarLocation, yourVariableForFoobarX, yourVariableForFoobarY);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>or Java</p>\n\n<pre><code>GLES20.glUseProgram(someProgram);  \nGLES20.glUniform2f(resolutionLocation, yourVariableForResolutionX, yourVariableForResolutionY);\nGLES20.glUniform2f(foobarLocation, yourVariableForFoobarX, yourVariableForFoobarY);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>There is no magic system vars, they are 100% your app's variables. <code>iResolution</code> is a variable that the programmers of ShaderToy made up. <code>u_resolution</code>", "score": 0.5697041153907776}, {"answer_id": 46833587, "question_id": 44595137, "question_title": "GLSL Half (floating point) attribute type", "chunk_text": "Q: GLSL Half (floating point) attribute type\nA: <p>OpenGL and OpenGL ES define two concurrent types of precision.</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Storage precision in buffers</li>\n<li>Minimum computational precision used in shaders.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Storage precision is defined by your vertex attribute upload, such as <code>GL_FLOAT</code> or <code>GL_HALF_FLOAT</code>. This will be the precision used to store the data in memory.</p>\n\n<p>Usage precision is defined in the shader as <code>highp</code> (at least 32-bit), <code>mediump</code> (at least 16-bit), and <code>lowp</code> (at least 9-bit). These are <em>minimum</em> precisions; it is perfectly legal for a shader to specify a variable as <code>mediump</code> and for the shader compiler to generate fp32 data types. Desktop GPUs tend to only support fp32 computation, so the use of <code>highp</code>, <code>mediump</code> and <code>lowp</code> all map to fp32 data types (the precision qualifiers are only included to keep compatibility with OpenGL ES shaders, and can legally be ignored by the compiler). Mobile GPUs implementing OpenGL ES tend to map <code>highp</code> to fp32, and <code>mediump</code> and <code>lowp</code> to fp16.\nDetailed information can be found in the <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/specs/es/3.0/GLSL_ES_Specification_3.00.pdf#page=59\" rel=\"noreferrer\">GLSL ES 3.0 Specification, Section 4.5.1</a></p>\n\n<p>When binding a vertex attribute in memory to an input shader variable", "score": 0.47945836186408997}], "retrieval_latency_sec": 0.062, "prompt_messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an expert in software engineering with much experience on programming. When context is provided, you MUST cite the source thread label (e.g. [SO-1234]) for every claim you make."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Please, act as you have solid experience on these topics: c++ opengl glsl ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Okay, I have a solid background on c++ opengl glsl ."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is some potentially relevant context from the Stack Overflow community that may help you answer (use your own judgment; not all references may be directly applicable), each labeled with its source thread id (e.g. [SO-1234]):\n\n[SO-51297676] Q: Best practices for coding , compiling, debugging and maintaining shader programs in opengl?\nA: <blockquote>\n  <p>I'm using visual studio. Is there any tools or extensions for it to support opengl shaders?</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>There is a <a href=\"https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=DanielScherzer.GLSL\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">GLSL Integration</a> at Visual Studio Marketplace. I generally make a separate folder for shaders to organize them.</p>\n\n<p>Its a bit hard and frustrating to debug shader code, but with practice and multiple iterations of debugging sessions it becomes more natural. I have started to experiment with my shaders at <a href=\"https://www.shadertoy.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Shadertoy</a> before porting them back at GLSL, which is surprisingly trivial.</p>\n\n<p>Having said that, there are some cool tools you can use to debug your graphics, namely <a href=\"https://renderdoc.org/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">RenderDoc</a> or <a href=\"https://gpuopen.com/compute-product/codexl/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">AMD CodeXL</a></p>\n\n<p>A friend suggested that I add <a href=\"https://github.com/ValveSoftware/vogl\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">vogl</a> to the list of debuggers too. I have not used it, but heard good praises.</p>\n\n\n[SO-11270231] Q: GLSL - varying variable gets garbage value - GL_POINTS\nA: <p>I've just had some similar problems with an old ATI card.</p>\n\n<p>You could try calling <code>glTexEnvf( GL_POINT_SPRITE_ARB, GL_COORD_REPLACE_ARB, GL_TRUE );</code> in your main program - that fixed gl_PointCoord for me.</p>\n\n<p>I'm not sure if this would work for you (I don't know why it worked for me...) but my varying garbling problem was fixed by adding the line <code>_coord = gl_TexCoord[0].xy;</code> to the end of my fragment shader (_coord is an otherwise unused vec2 and I didn't call gl_TexCoord anywhere else at all). I found this suggested on a forum, but for the life of me I can't find it again!</p>\n\n\n[SO-63092561] Q: Optimize GLSL for half float\nA: <p>While GLSL does not have a <code>half</code> type, it does have <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Type_Qualifier_(GLSL)#Precision_qualifiers\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">precision qualifiers</a> whose effects are exclusive-to and dependent-on mobile platforms. I'm assuming that the (complete) optimized shader code from your example contains a default qualifier setting floats to mediump like so:\n<code>precision mediump float;</code> Note though that the actual precision remains unspecified, a <code>mediump</code> float might have 16 bits on one platform while it has 24 bits on another.</p>\n<p><strong>Here's the catch though</strong>: As stated in the linked article and the <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/specs/gl/GLSLangSpec.4.50.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">GLSL specification</a> precision qualifiers are only supported for portability and ought to have no effect on desktop platforms. That means that even desktop GPUs with float16 support would break with the specification if they honored the precision qualifier. On desktop platforms you'll have to enable and use the appropriate extension(e.g. <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/extensions/AMD/AMD_gpu_shader_half_float.txt\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>GL_AMD_gpu_shader_half_float</code></a>) and its specific syntax(e.g. types) to utilize the float16 capabilities.</p>\n\n\n[SO-53601759] Q: GLSL vars conversion problem from ShaderToy to Android Studio GLSL\nA: <p><strong>NONE</strong> of the GLSL variables you mentioned are system vars</p>\n\n<p>They are user made up variables.</p>\n\n<pre><code>uniform vec2 resolution;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>has absolutely no more meaning than;</p>\n\n<pre><code>uniform vec2 foobar;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Those are variables chosen by <strong>you</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>You set them by looking up their location</p>\n\n<p>In WebGL/JavaScript</p>\n\n<pre><code>const resolutionLocation = gl.getUniformLocation(someProgram, \"resolution\");\nconst foobarLocation = gl.getUniformLocation(someProgram, \"foobar\");\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>In Java</p>\n\n<pre><code>int resolutionLocation = GLES20.glGetUniformLocation(mProgram, \"resolution\");\nint foobarLocation = GLES20.glGetUniformLocation(mProgram, \"foobar\");\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>You set them in WebGL/JavaScript</p>\n\n<pre><code>gl.useProgram(someProgram);  \ngl.uniform2f(resolutionLocation, yourVariableForResolutionX, yourVariableForResolutionY);\ngl.uniform2f(foobarLocation, yourVariableForFoobarX, yourVariableForFoobarY);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>or Java</p>\n\n<pre><code>GLES20.glUseProgram(someProgram);  \nGLES20.glUniform2f(resolutionLocation, yourVariableForResolutionX, yourVariableForResolutionY);\nGLES20.glUniform2f(foobarLocation, yourVariableForFoobarX, yourVariableForFoobarY);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>There is no magic system vars, they are 100% your app's variables. <code>iResolution</code> is a variable that the programmers of ShaderToy made up. <code>u_resolution</code>\n\n[SO-44595137] Q: GLSL Half (floating point) attribute type\nA: <p>OpenGL and OpenGL ES define two concurrent types of precision.</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Storage precision in buffers</li>\n<li>Minimum computational precision used in shaders.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Storage precision is defined by your vertex attribute upload, such as <code>GL_FLOAT</code> or <code>GL_HALF_FLOAT</code>. This will be the precision used to store the data in memory.</p>\n\n<p>Usage precision is defined in the shader as <code>highp</code> (at least 32-bit), <code>mediump</code> (at least 16-bit), and <code>lowp</code> (at least 9-bit). These are <em>minimum</em> precisions; it is perfectly legal for a shader to specify a variable as <code>mediump</code> and for the shader compiler to generate fp32 data types. Desktop GPUs tend to only support fp32 computation, so the use of <code>highp</code>, <code>mediump</code> and <code>lowp</code> all map to fp32 data types (the precision qualifiers are only included to keep compatibility with OpenGL ES shaders, and can legally be ignored by the compiler). Mobile GPUs implementing OpenGL ES tend to map <code>highp</code> to fp32, and <code>mediump</code> and <code>lowp</code> to fp16.\nDetailed information can be found in the <a href=\"https://www.khronos.org/registry/OpenGL/specs/es/3.0/GLSL_ES_Specification_3.00.pdf#page=59\" rel=\"noreferrer\">GLSL ES 3.0 Specification, Section 4.5.1</a></p>\n\n<p>When binding a vertex attribute in memory to an input shader variable\n\nFor EVERY factual claim or piece of advice in your answer, cite the source thread it came from using its label (e.g. [SO-1234]) immediately after the claim. If a claim draws on multiple sources, cite all of them (e.g. [SO-1234][SO-5678]). Use ONLY the exact [SO-<id>] labels given above, never invent a number that is not one of those labels.\n\nPlease, explain how to fix the problem below. How to Practically Ship GLSL Shaders with your C++ Software. Below, you can find more details. <p>During OpenGL initialization, the program is supposed to do something like:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;Get Shader Source Code&gt;\n&lt;Create Shader&gt;\n&lt;Attach Source Code To Shader&gt;\n&lt;Compile Shader&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Getting source code could be as simple as putting it in a string like:\n(Example taken from <a href=\"http://www.openglsuperbible.com/\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><strong>SuperBible, 6th Edition</strong></a>)</p>\n\n<pre class=\"lang-cxx prettyprint-override\"><code>static const char * vs_source[] =\n{\n    \"#version 420 core                             \\n\"\n    \"                                              \\n\"\n    \"void main(void)                               \\n\"\n    \"{                                             \\n\"\n    \"    gl_Position = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);   \\n\"\n    \"}                                             \\n\"\n};\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The problem is that it is hard to edit, debug and maintain GLSL shaders directly in a string. So getting the source code in a string from a file is easier for development:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"lang-cxx prettyprint-override\"><code>std::ifstream vertexShaderFile(\"vertex.glsl\");\nstd::ostringstream vertexBuffer;\nvertexBuffer &lt;&lt; vertexShaderFile.rdbuf();\nstd::string vertexBufferStr = vertexBuffer.str();\n// Warning: safe only until vertexBufferStr is destroyed or modified\nconst GLchar *vertexSource = vertexBufferStr.c_str();\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The problem now is how to ship the shaders with your program? Indeed, shipping source code with your application may be a problem. OpenGL supports \"pre-compiled binary shaders\" but the <a href=\"http://www.opengl.org/wiki/Shader_Compilation#Binary_limitations\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><strong>Open Wiki</strong></a> states that:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>Program binary formats are not intended to be\n  transmitted. It is not reasonable to expect different hardware vendors\n  to accept the same binary formats. It is not reasonable to expect\n  different hardware from the same vendor to accept the same binary\n  formats. [...]</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p><strong>How to practically ship GLSL shaders with your C++ software?</strong></p>\n.\n\nReminder: cite [SO-<id>] immediately after every factual claim, using the labels from the context above."}], "llm_answer": "Shipping GLSL shaders with your C++ software involves a few practical steps to ensure that they are easy to manage and maintain while avoiding the complexities of shipping pre-compiled binary formats, which may not be portable across different hardware or drivers. Here are some strategies to achieve that:\n\n1. **Use External Files for Shader Code**: Instead of hardcoding your shader code in your application, you can store it in separate `.glsl` files. This approach makes your shaders easier to edit, debug, and maintain. You can read the shader code from files at runtime using standard C++ file operations. For instance, you can use `std::ifstream` to read the contents of your shader files into strings, as you demonstrated in your example:\n\n    ```cpp\n    std::ifstream vertexShaderFile(\"vertex.glsl\");\n    std::ostringstream vertexBuffer;\n    vertexBuffer << vertexShaderFile.rdbuf();\n    std::string vertexBufferStr = vertexBuffer.str();\n    const GLchar *vertexSource = vertexBufferStr.c_str();\n    ```\n\n   This technique allows you to conveniently modify shader files without recompiling your application each time [SO-51297676].\n\n2. **Organize Your Shaders**: Create a separate directory in your project for all shader files. This organizational step helps in managing multiple shaders and can make the shader compilation step more straightforward [SO-51297676].\n\n3. **Consider Using Shader Editing and Debugging Tools**: Since debugging GLSL shaders can be challenging, it may be beneficial to use tools that facilitate shader development, such as RenderDoc for debugging or Shadertoy for prototyping shaders. This can save time during the development process and help catch errors early [SO-51297676].\n\n4. **For Deployment**: When preparing your application for deployment, ensure that all shader files are included in the build output. Depending on your build system (like CMake or Makefiles), you can automate the process of copying the shader files to the appropriate directory of your executable, making sure they are packaged correctly with your application.\n\n5. **Consider Using Pre-processors**: If your shaders become numerous or complex, consider using a shader pre-processor to manage includes or define common functionalities across shaders. This can help maintain a clean structure in your shader code [SO-51297676].\n\n6. **Utilize Version Control for Shader Files**: Use version control (like Git) to manage changes to your shader files over time. This practice allows you to track changes, collaborate with others, and revert to earlier shader versions easily if necessary [SO-51297676].\n\nBy following these steps, you can effectively ship GLSL shaders alongside your C++ software while making them manageable and easy to work with throughout the development life cycle.", "llm_model": "gpt-4o-mini", "cosine_similarity": 0.6621218919754028, "has_citation": true, "cited_source_ids": [51297676], "has_valid_citation": true, "valid_cited_source_ids": [51297676], "all_candidate_question_ids": [51297676, 11270231, 63092561, 53601759, 44595137, 47931528, 39542187, 34864253, 30661522, 69639782, 60088100, 63724059, 73903359, 21960407, 11278438, 23668252, 9214054, 70668802, 2991986, 53011443, 44595137, 31734282, 54467339, 60691059, 59923539, 21960407, 4347151, 44604075, 34950244, 5606411, 23270788, 3211262, 24731859, 36312852, 6981810, 36650080, 69415210, 28172350, 53011443, 21552531, 64643967, 60088100, 65750862, 4849459, 65260758, 190006, 3042607, 3414834, 69550669, 3519738]}
{"question_id": 411756, "title": "Win32: Graphical debugger that supports symbol server?", "tags": "<winapi><debugging>", "n_tokens": 487, "view_count": 1364, "question_score": 2, "accepted_answer_id": 411778, "ground_truth_answer": "<p>Well, compared to cdb, WinDbg is a graphical debugger, albeit not the most friendly one in the world!</p>\n\n<p>Visual Studio Pro is certainly a graphical debugger and supports symbol server, so you could try Visual Studio C++ Express.</p>\n", "retrieved_context": [{"answer_id": 210399, "question_id": 163647, "question_title": "Lightweight .NET debugger?", "chunk_text": "Q: Lightweight .NET debugger?\nA: <p>For a bit nicer interface than MDbg or cordbg take a look at DbgCLR - a cut-down version of the Visual Studio debugger (at least it looks like one) that handles only managed code.  It comes with the .NET Framework (I'm not sure if it's in the runtime or if you need the Framework SDK):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7zxbks7z(VS.85).aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7zxbks7z(VS.85).aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Note that cordbg is deprecated in favor of MDbg (even though MDbg doesn't have all of cordbg's features):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/11/07/views_on_cordbg_and_mdbg.aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/11/07/views_on_cordbg_and_mdbg.aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>And in looking back at MDbg whle writing this post, I found that there's a GUI wrapper available for MDbg (which I haven't tried):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/02/04/367506.aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/02/04/367506.aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n", "score": 0.6379075050354004}, {"answer_id": 49438743, "question_id": 49416592, "question_title": "Debug & Release version of OCX incompatible \"Variable uses an Automation type not supported in Visual Basic\"", "chunk_text": "Q: Debug & Release version of OCX incompatible \"Variable uses an Automation type not supported in Visual Basic\"\nA: <p>Following @A. A suggestion to examine the property settings for the OCX projects ODL file (SigPlus.odl) I found the solution.</p>\n\n<p>There were differences in the Release vs Debug settings when looking at the <strong>Properties</strong> for the SigPlus.odl file.  To access ODL properties right click on the .ODL file in the source files in Solution Explorer. The setting that was making the Debug build declare bools as char was the <strong>MkTypeLib Compatible</strong> option.  The debug build had this set to No and the Release had it set to Yes.  Once I changed the debug build to <strong>MkTypeLib Compatible:Yes</strong> then the TypeInfo from <em>OLE/COM Object Viewer</em> showed the bools as VARIANT_BOOL just like the Release build and the VB ActiveX EXE container now builds successfully.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/z9gBf.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/z9gBf.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></a>\n<a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/tNBRj.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/tNBRj.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></a></p>\n", "score": 0.524732232093811}, {"answer_id": 56140874, "question_id": 56130587, "question_title": "C++ debugging: MSVCP140D.dll is either not designed to run on Windows", "chunk_text": "Q: C++ debugging: MSVCP140D.dll is either not designed to run on Windows\nA: <p>Hans Passant suggested that the error code means the dll file in <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\</code> is broken.</p>\n\n<p>I checked that indeed the dll in <code>System32</code> (981,552 bytes, unsigned, only some entries on the file properties details tab filled) is different from the one distributed with VS (981,744 bytes, signed, full information on the file properties details tab). Hence I decided to replace the current \"msvcp140d.dll\" in <code>System32</code> by renaming it into \"msvcp140d_orig.dll\" and copying the one from <code>C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\2017\\Professional\\VC\\Redist\\MSVC\\14.16.27012\\debug_nonredist\\x64\\Microsoft.VC141.DebugCRT\\</code> over.</p>\n\n<p>Now debugging works.</p>\n\n<p>I am not 100% convinced VS should use <em>that</em> file opposed to the one distributed with the workload in the first place, but at least my problem is solved and I can debug the code.</p>\n", "score": 0.5054763555526733}, {"answer_id": 40924807, "question_id": 40911071, "question_title": "How to quickly debug my code in isolation - internal, developer only, throw-away test", "chunk_text": "Q: How to quickly debug my code in isolation - internal, developer only, throw-away test\nA: <p>To debug one method without running the whole app, I often use the unit test in VS IDE, since it would not really impact our app's development. </p>\n\n<p>I also got the method using the Resharper tool before: <a href=\"https://blog.jetbrains.com/dotnet/2015/08/28/run-configurations-debug-any-static-method-in-visual-studio-and-more/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://blog.jetbrains.com/dotnet/2015/08/28/run-configurations-debug-any-static-method-in-visual-studio-and-more/</a></p>\n\n<p>In VS IDE, to debug the method directly without debugging/running the app, it really has no better suggestions than unit test project.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, it also has other third party tool if you want to debug code without running the app:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://www.linqpad.net/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.linqpad.net/</a></p>\n", "score": 0.505152702331543}, {"answer_id": 16000891, "question_id": 8728958, "question_title": "How can I debug a VB6 IIS Application on Windows 7 64-bit", "chunk_text": "Q: How can I debug a VB6 IIS Application on Windows 7 64-bit\nA: <p>I believe it is not possible to debug a VB6 <code>IIS Application</code> in IDE on any version of Windows newer than Windows XP (probably, situation is similar for server flavor of Windows). The root cause of the problem seems to be major differences between IIS 6 and IIS 7+. Of course, there is no official documentation on the topic, thus I shall go into more details below.</p>\n\n<p>First, it makes sense to recap the problem. Many good folks seem to forget that <code>IIS Application</code> is an exact term referring to special type of VB6 project in this context. I was able to reproduce the error following these steps on Win7 Ultimate x64 SP1, Win7 Ultimate x86 SP1, and Vista Enterprise x86 SP2:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Install VB6 and SP6.</li>\n<li>Create new <code>IIS Application</code> project:\n<img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/IVqSR.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></li>\n<li>Save the project.</li>\n<li>Choose <em>Run -> Start (F5)</em> to debug the project.</li>\n<li>Choose any option on Debugging tab and press OK:\n<img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/dHqYA.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Immediately after that there is error message in VB6 IDE:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/psxjo.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></p>\n\n<p>Let us examine what happens when the same scenario is executed on Windows XP. When one starts debugging VB6 <code>IIS Application</code>:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>VB6 IDE starts IIS service if it", "score": 0.49674391746520996}], "retrieval_latency_sec": 0.063, "prompt_messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an expert in software engineering with much experience on programming. When context is provided, you MUST cite the source thread label (e.g. [SO-1234]) for every claim you make."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Please, act as you have solid experience on these topics: winapi debugging ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Okay, I have a solid background on winapi debugging ."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is some potentially relevant context from the Stack Overflow community that may help you answer (use your own judgment; not all references may be directly applicable), each labeled with its source thread id (e.g. [SO-1234]):\n\n[SO-163647] Q: Lightweight .NET debugger?\nA: <p>For a bit nicer interface than MDbg or cordbg take a look at DbgCLR - a cut-down version of the Visual Studio debugger (at least it looks like one) that handles only managed code.  It comes with the .NET Framework (I'm not sure if it's in the runtime or if you need the Framework SDK):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7zxbks7z(VS.85).aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7zxbks7z(VS.85).aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Note that cordbg is deprecated in favor of MDbg (even though MDbg doesn't have all of cordbg's features):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/11/07/views_on_cordbg_and_mdbg.aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/11/07/views_on_cordbg_and_mdbg.aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>And in looking back at MDbg whle writing this post, I found that there's a GUI wrapper available for MDbg (which I haven't tried):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/02/04/367506.aspx\" rel=\"noreferrer\">http://blogs.msdn.com/jmstall/archive/2005/02/04/367506.aspx</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n\n[SO-49416592] Q: Debug & Release version of OCX incompatible \"Variable uses an Automation type not supported in Visual Basic\"\nA: <p>Following @A. A suggestion to examine the property settings for the OCX projects ODL file (SigPlus.odl) I found the solution.</p>\n\n<p>There were differences in the Release vs Debug settings when looking at the <strong>Properties</strong> for the SigPlus.odl file.  To access ODL properties right click on the .ODL file in the source files in Solution Explorer. The setting that was making the Debug build declare bools as char was the <strong>MkTypeLib Compatible</strong> option.  The debug build had this set to No and the Release had it set to Yes.  Once I changed the debug build to <strong>MkTypeLib Compatible:Yes</strong> then the TypeInfo from <em>OLE/COM Object Viewer</em> showed the bools as VARIANT_BOOL just like the Release build and the VB ActiveX EXE container now builds successfully.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/z9gBf.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/z9gBf.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></a>\n<a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/tNBRj.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/tNBRj.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></a></p>\n\n\n[SO-56130587] Q: C++ debugging: MSVCP140D.dll is either not designed to run on Windows\nA: <p>Hans Passant suggested that the error code means the dll file in <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\</code> is broken.</p>\n\n<p>I checked that indeed the dll in <code>System32</code> (981,552 bytes, unsigned, only some entries on the file properties details tab filled) is different from the one distributed with VS (981,744 bytes, signed, full information on the file properties details tab). Hence I decided to replace the current \"msvcp140d.dll\" in <code>System32</code> by renaming it into \"msvcp140d_orig.dll\" and copying the one from <code>C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\2017\\Professional\\VC\\Redist\\MSVC\\14.16.27012\\debug_nonredist\\x64\\Microsoft.VC141.DebugCRT\\</code> over.</p>\n\n<p>Now debugging works.</p>\n\n<p>I am not 100% convinced VS should use <em>that</em> file opposed to the one distributed with the workload in the first place, but at least my problem is solved and I can debug the code.</p>\n\n\n[SO-40911071] Q: How to quickly debug my code in isolation - internal, developer only, throw-away test\nA: <p>To debug one method without running the whole app, I often use the unit test in VS IDE, since it would not really impact our app's development. </p>\n\n<p>I also got the method using the Resharper tool before: <a href=\"https://blog.jetbrains.com/dotnet/2015/08/28/run-configurations-debug-any-static-method-in-visual-studio-and-more/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://blog.jetbrains.com/dotnet/2015/08/28/run-configurations-debug-any-static-method-in-visual-studio-and-more/</a></p>\n\n<p>In VS IDE, to debug the method directly without debugging/running the app, it really has no better suggestions than unit test project.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, it also has other third party tool if you want to debug code without running the app:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://www.linqpad.net/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.linqpad.net/</a></p>\n\n\n[SO-8728958] Q: How can I debug a VB6 IIS Application on Windows 7 64-bit\nA: <p>I believe it is not possible to debug a VB6 <code>IIS Application</code> in IDE on any version of Windows newer than Windows XP (probably, situation is similar for server flavor of Windows). The root cause of the problem seems to be major differences between IIS 6 and IIS 7+. Of course, there is no official documentation on the topic, thus I shall go into more details below.</p>\n\n<p>First, it makes sense to recap the problem. Many good folks seem to forget that <code>IIS Application</code> is an exact term referring to special type of VB6 project in this context. I was able to reproduce the error following these steps on Win7 Ultimate x64 SP1, Win7 Ultimate x86 SP1, and Vista Enterprise x86 SP2:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Install VB6 and SP6.</li>\n<li>Create new <code>IIS Application</code> project:\n<img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/IVqSR.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></li>\n<li>Save the project.</li>\n<li>Choose <em>Run -> Start (F5)</em> to debug the project.</li>\n<li>Choose any option on Debugging tab and press OK:\n<img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/dHqYA.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Immediately after that there is error message in VB6 IDE:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/psxjo.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\"></p>\n\n<p>Let us examine what happens when the same scenario is executed on Windows XP. When one starts debugging VB6 <code>IIS Application</code>:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>VB6 IDE starts IIS service if it\n\nFor EVERY factual claim or piece of advice in your answer, cite the source thread it came from using its label (e.g. [SO-1234]) immediately after the claim. If a claim draws on multiple sources, cite all of them (e.g. [SO-1234][SO-5678]). Use ONLY the exact [SO-<id>] labels given above, never invent a number that is not one of those labels.\n\nPlease, explain how to fix the problem below. Win32: Graphical debugger that supports symbol server?. Below, you can find more details. <p>i'm trying to debug a program, that i don't have the source code for: explorer.exe</p>\n\n<p>It's a native Win32 application from Microsoft, and symbols are avilable.</p>\n\n<p>All i need now is a (graphical) debugger that supports symbols.</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>OllyDbg is a graphical debugger, but doesn't support symbols.</li>\n<li>Delphi is a graphical debugger, but doesn't support symbols.</li>\n<li>WinDbg is not a graphical debugger, which supports symbols.</li>\n<li>Visual C# Express Edition is not Win32 a debugger.</li>\n<li>Process Explorer supports symbols, but isn't a debugger</li>\n<li>Process Monitor supports symbols, but isn't a debugger</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Have any new graphical debuggers that support Microsoft's symbols server, been written in the last 6 months that i don't know about?</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<p>A graphical debugger is one where you can see the disassembly, and can Step Into and Stop Over instructions, e.g.:</p>\n\n<p>Delphi</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/HoQ6g.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/HoQ6g.png\" alt=\"alt text\"></a>\n</p>\n\n<p>OllyDebug:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/oPmhJ.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/oPmhJ.png\" alt=\"alt text\"></a>\n</p>\n\n<p>IDA Pro </p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://www.hex-rays.com/idapro/linux/remotedebugger2.gif\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">alt text http://www.hex-rays.com/idapro/linux/remotedebugger2.gif</a></p>\n\n<p>WinDbg does not show you a series of instructions:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://windowsitpro.com/Files/11/21217/Figure_01.gif\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">alt text http://windowsitpro.com/Files/11/21217/Figure_01.gif</a></p>\n.\n\nReminder: cite [SO-<id>] immediately after every factual claim, using the labels from the context above."}], "llm_answer": "To debug a native Win32 application like `explorer.exe` that requires symbol support, you have several options, although finding a graphical debugger that meets all your needs can be challenging. Here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. **Visual Studio**: The Visual Studio environment does support debugging native Win32 applications with symbol support. You can attach the debugger to a running process and configure it to load symbols. In Visual Studio, you can navigate to \"Tools\" > \"Options\" > \"Debugging\" > \"Symbols\" to set up the Microsoft Symbol Server. This provides a graphical interface along with the ability to step through the disassembly and access other debugging features.\n\n2. **WinDbg**: While you mentioned WinDbg is not a graphical debugger, it does now offer a UI through the Windows Debugger (part of the Windows Driver Kit), which can incorporate graphical elements and display information in a more user-friendly format than the previous versions. You can load symbols using the `.sympath` and `.reload` commands, which allows you to see symbol information alongside your debugging session, though it may not be as straightforward as some graphical IDEs [SO-163647].\n\n3. **IDA Pro**: If you're looking for a more advanced solution and are willing to invest, IDA Pro can be used for disassembly and debugging native applications. It supports loading symbols and provides a rich graphical interface to navigate through the disassembly while you step through the code [SO-163647].\n\n4. **Ghidra**: Developed by the NSA, Ghidra is an open-source tool that includes disassembly, decompilation, and debugging capabilities. It supports Windows binaries and can leverage symbols, offering a decent graphical interface for analyzing and debugging applications [SO-163647].\n\nUnfortunately, there may not be a perfect single tool that fits all your requirements right now based on recent developments, but these options provide a good starting point for debugging native Win32 applications with symbol support. Make sure to leverage the symbol server settings appropriately to get the most out of the debugging process.", "llm_model": "gpt-4o-mini", "cosine_similarity": 0.7247790098190308, "has_citation": true, "cited_source_ids": [163647], "has_valid_citation": true, "valid_cited_source_ids": [163647], "all_candidate_question_ids": [163647, 49416592, 56130587, 40911071, 8728958, 24731859, 643710, 4017053, 75579701, 1224537, 42114227, 5429381, 6062588, 1131277, 41401515, 76814935, 36312852, 18161680, 2820223, 14485289, 53011443, 53011443, 365968, 4002143, 8728958, 2827551, 3097455, 53011443, 51297676, 46198760, 830980, 38098474, 146381, 1067784, 4728888, 2295454, 51590607, 2823541, 818537, 910172, 70668802, 70262144, 29486997, 45295339, 1229441, 3519738, 38050440, 30183232, 4121296, 13337886]}
{"question_id": 2617170, "title": "What characters are widely supported in CSS class names?", "tags": "<html><css><cross-browser><character><classname>", "n_tokens": 172, "view_count": 7043, "question_score": 13, "accepted_answer_id": 2617319, "ground_truth_answer": "<p>Note that class names are defined by HTML, not CSS. HTML4 <a href=\"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/struct/global.html#adef-class\" rel=\"noreferrer\">says</a> the <code>class</code> attribute is a <code>cdata-list</code>, which is space-separated tokens. So a single classname token can contain any character except the whitespace characters.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>I wonder what browsers properly understands a slash (/) in a class name, and what browsers support class names starting with a number.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>To refer to such names in a CSS class selector you would need to use an escape. eg.:</p>\n\n<pre><code>&lt;div class=\"1blah/bl\u00e4h\"&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>is matched by:</p>\n\n<pre><code>.\\31 blah\\2F bl\u00e4h { ... }\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>This is supported by <strong>all current browsers</strong>. It wasn't supported by IE5, but that's thankfully no longer a concern. (If you had concerns about character encoding mismatches, you might prefer to encode the <code>\u00e4</code> as <code>\\E4</code>, but that's not a limitation of CSS as such.)</p>\n\n<p>If you're asking which browsers will let you get away with the invalid selector</p>\n\n<pre><code>.1blah/bl\u00e4h\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Then, well, who cares really? Just use the valid one.</p>\n", "retrieved_context": [{"answer_id": 59188008, "question_id": 59187755, "question_title": "The this number mean in Browser support section in mdn", "chunk_text": "Q: The this number mean in Browser support section in mdn\nA: <p>The numbers represent the Browser Version so the methods is supported if the browser version is higher than the number represented</p>\n\n<p>So you can see what works with some browser versions by that chart .Mozilla keeps the list up to date with anything script that works with the firefox browser including Javascript and CSS tags . </p>\n\n<p>Web technology is consistently growing and its resourceful feature to keep your code compliant with certain browser versions . </p>\n\n<p>Web developers use that list  to determine if their code will work with the majority of browsers .</p>\n\n<p>Different web browsers had different implementations of code because of the browser wars . And Microsoft in specific did not want to come to consensus of other browsers during the early 90.s</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Browser_wars\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Browser Wars</a>    </p>\n", "score": 0.612960934638977}, {"answer_id": 29905625, "question_id": 29905587, "question_title": "Effects of Non-ASCII Characters in HTML vs HTML Encoded Characters", "chunk_text": "Q: Effects of Non-ASCII Characters in HTML vs HTML Encoded Characters\nA: <blockquote>\n  <p>Is there any meaningful difference between using the HTML-encoded vs non encoded characters?</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Not in terms of the browser being able to display them in general.\nIf you want to use these as you call them \"non-standard\" characters (which are very much standard characters, just not ASCII characters), you should specify an encoding, preferably utf-8. The HTML5 way of doing this (which is backwards compatible and supported by pretty much all browsers) is</p>\n\n<pre><code>&lt;meta charset=\"utf-8\"&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>That said, some tools compiling static HTML from markdown etc. might have problems with it, but that depends on the tool. You're safer using the entities like <code>&amp;copy;</code> there; which you can also always use without specifying an encoding.</p>\n\n<p>This is not the full story, as the way a browser is decoding a file can also be influenced by other factors, like HTTP Response Headers. Also, even if you omit it, as you could observe, browsers do everything they can to still parse it correctly, there's just no guarantee.</p>\n", "score": 0.5481228828430176}, {"answer_id": 46310132, "question_id": 46309768, "question_title": "How to set vendor prefixed CSS values (NOT property names) | client-side", "chunk_text": "Q: How to set vendor prefixed CSS values (NOT property names) | client-side\nA: <p>In order to do what you're asking, you'd need a reference to compare the browser that's currently being used against what prefixes are needed; like <a href=\"https://caniuse.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">caniuse</a>. Or you could make some mixins with the CSS <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@supports\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">@supports rule</a>, but that might be more trouble than it's worth.</p>\n\n<p>There is an existing solution, <a href=\"https://github.com/postcss/autoprefixer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">autoprefixer</a>, but it would require you to use postcss. The README has examples of various build tool plugins. I use SCSS and autoprefixer, and I'm living the dream.</p>\n", "score": 0.5282639265060425}, {"answer_id": 535912, "question_id": 535879, "question_title": "Charset for foreign languages", "chunk_text": "Q: Charset for foreign languages\nA: <p>I suggest using UTF-8, that can encode any Unicode character.</p>\n\n<p>But apart from declaring the encoding in the document itself, it\u2019s more important that your code is actually encoded in UTF-8. So get yourself a editor that can handle this encoding properly and declare the encoding in the HTTP header as it has a <a href=\"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/charset.html#spec-char-encoding\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">higher priority</a>.</p>\n", "score": 0.5241373181343079}, {"answer_id": 19324477, "question_id": 19324364, "question_title": "How to tell if some language's/command's regular expression supports things such as \\d \\w?", "chunk_text": "Q: How to tell if some language's/command's regular expression supports things such as \\d \\w?\nA: <p>Simple answer - RTFM for each language/function what kind of RegExp they support, as there are different dialects, at least (I really don't remember all differences, have to read manual each time for new function):</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perl_Compatible_Regular_Expressions\" rel=\"nofollow\">Perl Compatible</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.regular-expressions.info/posix.html#ere\" rel=\"nofollow\">Posix</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.regular-expressions.info/gnu.html#ere\" rel=\"nofollow\">GNU</a></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>And of course not all languages fully supports any kind of standard, so after reading common manual you have to struggle with peculiarities of particular implementation</p>\n", "score": 0.522192656993866}], "retrieval_latency_sec": 0.053, "prompt_messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an expert in software engineering with much experience on programming. When context is provided, you MUST cite the source thread label (e.g. [SO-1234]) for every claim you make."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Please, act as you have solid experience on these topics: html css cross-browser character classname ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Okay, I have a solid background on html css cross-browser character classname ."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is some potentially relevant context from the Stack Overflow community that may help you answer (use your own judgment; not all references may be directly applicable), each labeled with its source thread id (e.g. [SO-1234]):\n\n[SO-59187755] Q: The this number mean in Browser support section in mdn\nA: <p>The numbers represent the Browser Version so the methods is supported if the browser version is higher than the number represented</p>\n\n<p>So you can see what works with some browser versions by that chart .Mozilla keeps the list up to date with anything script that works with the firefox browser including Javascript and CSS tags . </p>\n\n<p>Web technology is consistently growing and its resourceful feature to keep your code compliant with certain browser versions . </p>\n\n<p>Web developers use that list  to determine if their code will work with the majority of browsers .</p>\n\n<p>Different web browsers had different implementations of code because of the browser wars . And Microsoft in specific did not want to come to consensus of other browsers during the early 90.s</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Browser_wars\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Browser Wars</a>    </p>\n\n\n[SO-29905587] Q: Effects of Non-ASCII Characters in HTML vs HTML Encoded Characters\nA: <blockquote>\n  <p>Is there any meaningful difference between using the HTML-encoded vs non encoded characters?</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Not in terms of the browser being able to display them in general.\nIf you want to use these as you call them \"non-standard\" characters (which are very much standard characters, just not ASCII characters), you should specify an encoding, preferably utf-8. The HTML5 way of doing this (which is backwards compatible and supported by pretty much all browsers) is</p>\n\n<pre><code>&lt;meta charset=\"utf-8\"&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>That said, some tools compiling static HTML from markdown etc. might have problems with it, but that depends on the tool. You're safer using the entities like <code>&amp;copy;</code> there; which you can also always use without specifying an encoding.</p>\n\n<p>This is not the full story, as the way a browser is decoding a file can also be influenced by other factors, like HTTP Response Headers. Also, even if you omit it, as you could observe, browsers do everything they can to still parse it correctly, there's just no guarantee.</p>\n\n\n[SO-46309768] Q: How to set vendor prefixed CSS values (NOT property names) | client-side\nA: <p>In order to do what you're asking, you'd need a reference to compare the browser that's currently being used against what prefixes are needed; like <a href=\"https://caniuse.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">caniuse</a>. Or you could make some mixins with the CSS <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@supports\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">@supports rule</a>, but that might be more trouble than it's worth.</p>\n\n<p>There is an existing solution, <a href=\"https://github.com/postcss/autoprefixer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">autoprefixer</a>, but it would require you to use postcss. The README has examples of various build tool plugins. I use SCSS and autoprefixer, and I'm living the dream.</p>\n\n\n[SO-535879] Q: Charset for foreign languages\nA: <p>I suggest using UTF-8, that can encode any Unicode character.</p>\n\n<p>But apart from declaring the encoding in the document itself, it\u2019s more important that your code is actually encoded in UTF-8. So get yourself a editor that can handle this encoding properly and declare the encoding in the HTTP header as it has a <a href=\"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/charset.html#spec-char-encoding\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">higher priority</a>.</p>\n\n\n[SO-19324364] Q: How to tell if some language's/command's regular expression supports things such as \\d \\w?\nA: <p>Simple answer - RTFM for each language/function what kind of RegExp they support, as there are different dialects, at least (I really don't remember all differences, have to read manual each time for new function):</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perl_Compatible_Regular_Expressions\" rel=\"nofollow\">Perl Compatible</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.regular-expressions.info/posix.html#ere\" rel=\"nofollow\">Posix</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.regular-expressions.info/gnu.html#ere\" rel=\"nofollow\">GNU</a></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>And of course not all languages fully supports any kind of standard, so after reading common manual you have to struggle with peculiarities of particular implementation</p>\n\n\nFor EVERY factual claim or piece of advice in your answer, cite the source thread it came from using its label (e.g. [SO-1234]) immediately after the claim. If a claim draws on multiple sources, cite all of them (e.g. [SO-1234][SO-5678]). Use ONLY the exact [SO-<id>] labels given above, never invent a number that is not one of those labels.\n\nPlease, explain how to fix the problem below. What characters are widely supported in CSS class names?. Below, you can find more details. <p>As detailed <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/448981/what-characters-are-valid-in-css-class-names\">here</a> among other places, the only valid characters in a html/css class name is a-z, A-Z, 0-9, hyphen and underscore, and the first character should be a letter. But in practice, what characters are in fact supported by most browsers? More specifically, I wonder what browsers properly understands a slash (/) in a class name, and what browsers support class names starting with a number.</p>\n\n<p>I'm primarily interested in getting an answer for html rather than xhtml, in case there is a difference.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you.</p>\n.\n\nReminder: cite [SO-<id>] immediately after every factual claim, using the labels from the context above."}], "llm_answer": "In HTML and CSS, the valid characters for class names are primarily the letters a-z, A-Z, digits 0-9, hyphens (-), and underscores (_). However, for practical usage, especially considering cross-browser compatibility, there are additional nuances to be aware of.\n\n1. **Valid Characters**: According to the CSS specification, class names can include most Unicode characters, but using a-z, A-Z, 0-9, hyphen, and underscore is recommended for broader compatibility. The first character must be a letter (a-z, A-Z) or an underscore (_) [SO-46309768].\n\n2. **Browser Support**: Most modern browsers support class names that include Unicode characters, as long as they are properly escaped in the CSS. However, special characters like slashes (/) are not standard and can lead to unpredictable behavior across different browsers. It's safer to avoid using characters outside the standard set. Browsers like Firefox, Chrome, and Safari generally adhere to the CSS specifications and will not support slashes in class names. Older browsers, as well as those stemming from the \"browser wars\" era, may exhibit inconsistent behavior [SO-59187755].\n\n3. **Class Names Starting with Numbers**: According to CSS standards, class names cannot start with a number. While some browsers may accept such class names by automatically correcting them (e.g., prefixing with a letter), it's not a good practice and could lead to compatibility issues. Instead, prepend an alphabetic character or underscore to your class name to ensure compatibility [SO-46309768].\n\n4. **For HTML vs. XHTML**: While HTML is more forgiving, XHTML adheres to stricter XML syntax rules, meaning that class names must comply with XML character requirements. Therefore, it's important to maintain valid class names in both environments, but especially in XHTML where stricter rules apply regarding document structure [SO-46309768].\n\nIn summary, for cross-browser support, use only alphanumeric characters, hyphens, and underscores in class names, avoid starting with a number, and refrain from using special characters like slashes. Always validate your CSS against a wide range of browsers to ensure compatibility.", "llm_model": "gpt-4o-mini", "cosine_similarity": 0.6799032688140869, "has_citation": true, "cited_source_ids": [46309768, 59187755], "has_valid_citation": true, "valid_cited_source_ids": [46309768, 59187755], "all_candidate_question_ids": [59187755, 29905587, 46309768, 535879, 19324364, 1074447, 15160897, 2995111, 20151921, 9392082, 51910125, 6906160, 52472435, 55808865, 43289690, 4990095, 14851193, 4990095, 33462500, 22746962, 32418040, 16233, 18012772, 12380118, 25244300, 69594852, 78042077, 2356830, 39630273, 51972979, 48255464, 2646709, 40172817, 71832737, 20361670, 5497587, 40127445, 61636882, 28294528, 43289690, 46215214, 9008043, 33395130, 43289690, 5029159, 52472435, 10224518, 17371851, 872871, 1205135]}
