ch: \" + i);\n        }\n\n    }\n\n    public static void populateObjects() {\n", "score": 0.4289221167564392}], "retrieval_latency_sec": 0.061, "prompt_messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an expert in software engineering with much experience on programming. When context is provided, you MUST cite the source thread label (e.g. [SO-1234]) for every claim you make."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Please, act as you have solid experience on these topics: java c build ocaml ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Okay, I have a solid background on java c build ocaml ."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is some potentially relevant context from the Stack Overflow community that may help you answer (use your own judgment; not all references may be directly applicable), each labeled with its source thread id (e.g. [SO-1234]):\n\n[SO-183882] Q: Interesting compiler projects\nA: <p>Other interesting projects might include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><p>Add a tail-call optimization to the open-source Sun JVM.</p></li>\n<li><p>Add a named return value optimization (NRVO) to the Python or Ruby VM's.</p></li>\n<li><p>Add just-in-time regex-to-bytecode compilation, for your favorite target (.Net already has it. I'm pretty sure Java doesn't.)</p></li>\n</ul>\n\n\n[SO-60088100] > the \u00b5C, you could </p>\n\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>void call_0x0()\n{\n    callFunctionAt ((func_t) 0x0);\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>but whether this works depends on where your vector table is located, or more specifically, how <code>EIND</code> was initialized by the startup code.  What will always work is using a symbol and define it with <code>-Wl,--defsym,func=0</code> when linking with the following code:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>extern \"C\" void func();\n\nvoid call_func ()\n{\n    void (*addr)(void) = func;\n    callFunctionAt (addr);\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The big difference compared to using <code>0x0</code> directly it that the c